  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 223 PM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis afternoon there is an upper-level ridge over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow temperatures to \nwarm up with mostly dry weather. On Tuesday the upper-level ridge \nremains over North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This \nwill allow temperatures to warm up to above seasonal averages. \nTuesday evening an upper-level shortwave will move through the ridge \nand bring showers and thunderstorms to Southwestern Montana. On \nWednesday an upper-level trough begins to move over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow temperatures to \ncool down slightly with showers and thunderstorms across the area. \nDue to strong southerly flow aloft and strong low-level winds it \nwill be windy across North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana on Wednesday with the strongest winds along the Rocky \nMountain Front and in north-south orientated valleys. \n\nOn Thursday the upper-level trough remains over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This combined with strong \nsoutherly flow aloft and strong low-level winds will bring another \nday of windy conditions to the area. On Thursday there will be \nanother day of showers and thunderstorms. On Friday an upper-level \nlow is over Washington and Oregon with southwest flow over North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow \ntemperatures to be around seasonal averages with another day of \nshowers and thunderstorms and breezy conditions. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nOn Wednesday and Thursday there is a 40 - 80% chance for wind gusts \nof 45 mph or greater across Southwestern Montana. On Wednesday and \nThursday there is greater than a 50% chance for sustained winds of 40 \nmph or greater in the Madison River Valley. As a result a High Wind \nWatch was issued for the Madison River Valley from Wednesday \nafternoon through Thursday evening. On Wednesday and Thursday there \nis a 30 - 60% chance for wind gusts of 35 mph or greater across \nNorth-central Montana. On Friday there is a 40 - 80% chance for wind \ngusts of 30 mph or greater across North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana with the greatest probability in Southwestern \nMontana and along the Rocky Mountain Front. \n\nOn Tuesday there is a 15 - 30% chance for thunderstorms across North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. On Wednesday there is a \n15 - 30% chance for thunderstorms across most of North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. On Friday there is a 15 - 30% \nchance for thunderstorms across Southwestern Montana. On Tuesday \nthere is a 50% chance for a quarter inch of rain or greater across \nNorthwestern Beaverhead County.\n\nOn Saturday and Sunday there are significant differences in the \ntrack of the upper-level pattern in the deterministic models and \nensembles. One scenario is that the closed low remains situated \nover Washington/Oregon. This would keep status quo weather with \nseasonal temperatures and precipitation chances. Another scenario \nis that the closed upper-level low splits with one part going \nsouth and the other portion going up into Canada. This would bring\nbreezy conditons to Hi-Line with some precipitation primarily \nconfined to the Hi-Line. A third scenario is that the upper-level \nlow moves east and weakens some. This would bring cooler and \nwetter conditions to North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. -IG",
